1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,877
Here's a question that sounds easy.

2
00:00:02,197 --> 00:00:04,693
How many different games of tic-tac-toe are
there?

3
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:07,749
By a game, we mean the whole list of moves,
in order.

4
00:00:08,069 --> 00:00:10,364
So if two games end with the same board,

5
00:00:10,364 --> 00:00:14,896
but the moves happened in a different order,
they count as two different games.

6
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:17,861
Flipped or turned versions count separately
too.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,613
Take a guess. A hundred? A million?

8
00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:25,195
Pause the video and write your guess down.

9
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:37,572
The answer is 255,168.

10
00:00:37,892 --> 00:00:41,384
In this video we'll find out where that
number comes from:

11
00:00:41,384 --> 00:00:42,950
first by careful counting,

12
00:00:42,950 --> 00:00:47,044
and then with a short computer program that
plays every single game.

13
00:00:48,083 --> 00:00:50,024
Let's start with a simpler question.

14
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:52,746
Suppose the players ignore three in a row,

15
00:00:52,746 --> 00:00:55,720
and just keep going until all nine squares
are full.

16
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,686
In how many different orders can the board
fill up?

17
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:01,813
X goes first and has 9 squares to choose
from.

18
00:01:02,133 --> 00:01:04,864
For each of those, O has 8 squares left.

19
00:01:05,184 --> 00:01:08,586
That's 9 groups of 8, so the choices
multiply:

20
00:01:08,586 --> 00:01:12,949
9 times 8 is 72 different ways to make the
first two moves.

21
00:01:13,183 --> 00:01:18,132
Keep going: 9 times 8 times 7, and so on,
all the way down to 1.

22
00:01:18,452 --> 00:01:21,186
Mathematicians have a short name for this:

23
00:01:21,186 --> 00:01:24,831
nine factorial, written as a 9 with an
exclamation mark.

24
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:28,735
It equals 362,880.

25
00:01:28,983 --> 00:01:34,146
But wait. That's more than 255,168.

26
00:01:34,466 --> 00:01:35,916
Our count is too high.

27
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:38,476
Where are all those extra games coming from?

28
00:01:40,117 --> 00:01:41,184
Here's the catch.

29
00:01:41,504 --> 00:01:45,109
Real tic-tac-toe stops as soon as someone
gets three in a row.

30
00:01:45,429 --> 00:01:50,101
In this game, X wins on the fifth move, so
the last four squares are never played.

31
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:54,806
But our 362,880 counted those missing moves
anyway,

32
00:01:54,806 --> 00:01:58,563
as if the players kept going after the win.

33
00:01:58,883 --> 00:02:04,601
The 4 empty squares can be filled in 4 times
3 times 2 times 1, so 24 ways.

34
00:02:04,921 --> 00:02:07,609
So this one game got counted 24 times!

35
00:02:07,929 --> 00:02:10,467
Let's call those made-up endings ghost
games.

36
00:02:10,787 --> 00:02:13,369
We want to count each real game only once.

37
00:02:13,617 --> 00:02:15,046
So when can a game end?

38
00:02:15,366 --> 00:02:16,753
Not before move 5.

39
00:02:17,073 --> 00:02:18,804
X plays moves 1, 3 and 5,

40
00:02:18,804 --> 00:02:23,238
so the fifth move is the first time anyone
can have three marks.

41
00:02:23,484 --> 00:02:27,409
Pause and ponder: how many games end with X
winning on move 5?

42
00:02:27,729 --> 00:02:29,670
Try to count them before I show you.

43
00:02:49,917 --> 00:02:51,752
Here's one way, in three steps.

44
00:02:52,072 --> 00:02:54,205
First, pick the line X wins with.

45
00:02:54,525 --> 00:02:58,173
There are 8: 3 rows, 3 columns and 2
diagonals.

46
00:02:58,493 --> 00:03:01,557
Second, X fills that line in some order:

47
00:03:01,557 --> 00:03:05,618
3 times 2 times 1, which is 3 factorial, so
6 orders.

48
00:03:05,938 --> 00:03:10,312
Third, O's first mark can go on any of the
other 6 squares,

49
00:03:10,312 --> 00:03:14,984
and O's second mark on any of the 5 left: 6
times 5 is 30 ways.

50
00:03:15,304 --> 00:03:20,573
Multiply: 8 times 6 times 30 is 1,440 games.

51
00:03:22,417 --> 00:03:24,721
What about games that O wins on move 6?

52
00:03:25,041 --> 00:03:26,534
O needs three in a row.

53
00:03:26,854 --> 00:03:30,222
But careful: if X's three marks also make a
line,

54
00:03:30,222 --> 00:03:34,897
X already won on move 5, and the game
stopped before O could finish.

55
00:03:35,134 --> 00:03:40,030
Use the same three steps: 8 lines for O, 6
orders for O's marks,

56
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:44,009
and 6 times 5 times 4 ways to place X's
three marks.

57
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:48,382
That's 5,760 ways for O to finish a line.

58
00:03:48,702 --> 00:03:53,590
Then we subtract the 432 where X's three
marks made a line too,

59
00:03:53,590 --> 00:03:58,323
a row or column parallel to O's, so X
sneaked in a win first.

60
00:03:58,643 --> 00:04:01,523
That leaves 5,328 games.

61
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:05,796
For games that end on moves 7, 8 and 9, it
gets much worse:

62
00:04:05,796 --> 00:04:09,074
we'd have to check every earlier move for a
win,

63
00:04:09,074 --> 00:04:11,943
and a full board might be a win or a draw.

64
00:04:12,263 --> 00:04:14,055
It turns into a tangled mess.

65
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:17,180
When counting gets this messy, there's
another plan.

66
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:18,844
Don't count cleverly.

67
00:04:19,164 --> 00:04:24,455
Instead, teach a computer the rules, and let
it play every possible game, one by one.

68
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:27,505
First, the computer needs a board.

69
00:04:27,825 --> 00:04:29,511
We'll use a list of 9 squares.

70
00:04:29,831 --> 00:04:32,774
In Python, the programming language we're
using,

71
00:04:32,774 --> 00:04:35,962
the spots in a list are numbered starting
from zero,

72
00:04:35,962 --> 00:04:38,108
so our squares are numbered 0 to 8.

73
00:04:38,428 --> 00:04:40,199
An empty square holds a dot.

74
00:04:41,417 --> 00:04:44,489
Next, the computer needs to know what counts
as a win.

75
00:04:44,809 --> 00:04:48,990
We simply list all 8 winning lines, as
groups of three square numbers.

76
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:51,892
Squares 0, 1 and 2 make the top row.

77
00:04:52,212 --> 00:04:54,985
Squares 0, 4 and 8 make a diagonal.

78
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:59,807
Next comes a function: a little mini-program
with a name.

79
00:05:00,127 --> 00:05:02,473
This one, called winner, checks every line.

80
00:05:02,793 --> 00:05:05,795
If all three squares on a line hold the same
mark,

81
00:05:05,795 --> 00:05:08,617
and that mark isn't a dot, that player has
won.

82
00:05:08,937 --> 00:05:14,036
If no line matches, winner finds nothing,
and hands back None, which means nobody.

83
00:05:14,983 --> 00:05:18,034
Now for the clever part: a function called
explore.

84
00:05:18,354 --> 00:05:19,936
We tell it whose turn it is,

85
00:05:19,936 --> 00:05:24,455
and its job is to count every game that can
still happen from the current board.

86
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:26,599
It starts with two stopping rules.

87
00:05:26,919 --> 00:05:29,951
If someone has already won, that's one
finished game,

88
00:05:29,951 --> 00:05:31,953
so explore hands back the number 1.

89
00:05:32,273 --> 00:05:36,668
If the board is full with no winner, that's
a draw, also one finished game.

90
00:05:36,916 --> 00:05:39,028
Otherwise, the game isn't over yet.

91
00:05:39,348 --> 00:05:41,524
So explore tries every empty square.

92
00:05:41,844 --> 00:05:45,948
It puts the player's mark there, and then
asks itself the same question,

93
00:05:45,948 --> 00:05:49,481
now for the other player: how many games can
happen from here?

94
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:51,444
It adds up all the answers.

95
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:54,751
Let's zoom into the first of those, with O
to move.

96
00:05:55,071 --> 00:05:58,506
If O takes the top-right square, O wins:
that's 1 game.

97
00:05:58,826 --> 00:06:01,578
Erase it, and try the top-middle square
instead.

98
00:06:01,898 --> 00:06:05,162
Then X fills the last square, and it's a
draw: 1 more.

99
00:06:05,482 --> 00:06:08,191
Undo both moves, and add up: 2 games.

100
00:06:08,511 --> 00:06:12,180
With the other two branches, 2 plus 1 plus 2
makes 5.

101
00:06:12,416 --> 00:06:14,187
When a function calls itself,

102
00:06:14,187 --> 00:06:18,155
asking itself the same question like this,
it's called recursive.

103
00:06:18,475 --> 00:06:20,948
That might sound like it goes on forever,

104
00:06:20,948 --> 00:06:25,109
but each call adds one more mark, so a
stopping rule always kicks in.

105
00:06:25,429 --> 00:06:27,136
Picture an upside-down tree.

106
00:06:27,456 --> 00:06:31,308
The empty board is at the top, every
possible move is a branch,

107
00:06:31,308 --> 00:06:33,387
and every finished game is a leaf.

108
00:06:33,707 --> 00:06:38,507
Explore walks down every branch, one at a
time, and counts the leaves it reaches.

109
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:41,182
There's one more line, and it's easy to
miss.

110
00:06:41,502 --> 00:06:45,769
The program plays every game on just one
board, like a single whiteboard.

111
00:06:46,089 --> 00:06:49,161
So after exploring a move, it erases that
mark.

112
00:06:49,481 --> 00:06:53,406
That's called undoing the move, and it means
the next branch starts fresh.

113
00:06:53,726 --> 00:06:58,120
Trying a path, then stepping back to try the
next one, is called backtracking.

114
00:07:00,267 --> 00:07:03,936
A great way to understand a program is to
break it on purpose.

115
00:07:04,256 --> 00:07:08,416
Pause and ponder: what do you think happens
if we delete the undo line?

116
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:20,293
The program prints just 3.

117
00:07:20,613 --> 00:07:23,002
Without undo, nothing ever gets erased.

118
00:07:23,322 --> 00:07:27,102
The program plays one game, filling the
squares in order,

119
00:07:27,102 --> 00:07:30,575
until X makes a diagonal on move 7. That's
1.

120
00:07:30,895 --> 00:07:34,629
Then it tries X's other choices, but the old
X is still there.

121
00:07:34,949 --> 00:07:37,287
X lands on the bottom-middle square,

122
00:07:37,287 --> 00:07:40,730
and the old diagonal counts as a win again:
that's 2.

123
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:43,247
Then the bottom-right square: that's 3.

124
00:07:43,567 --> 00:07:46,554
Now the board is full, so there's nothing
left to try.

125
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,102
Now put undo back, and delete the winner
check instead,

126
00:07:50,102 --> 00:07:52,624
so games only stop when the board is full.

127
00:07:52,944 --> 00:07:54,630
Pause and predict the answer.

128
00:08:04,867 --> 00:08:07,768
362,880.

129
00:08:08,088 --> 00:08:09,518
That's nine factorial!

130
00:08:09,838 --> 00:08:13,385
Without the winner check, the program keeps
playing after a win,

131
00:08:13,385 --> 00:08:15,214
so it counts all the ghost games.

132
00:08:15,534 --> 00:08:17,667
It rediscovered our very first count.

133
00:08:18,717 --> 00:08:21,917
Now let's put every line back and run the
real program.

134
00:08:22,237 --> 00:08:26,760
After a moment, it prints 255,168.

135
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,624
Let's split that total by when each game
ends.

136
00:08:29,944 --> 00:08:38,733
1,440 end on move 5, and 5,328 on move 6,
the same numbers we found by hand.

137
00:08:39,053 --> 00:08:45,475
Then the bars shoot up: about 48 thousand
end on move 7, and 73 thousand on move 8.

138
00:08:45,795 --> 00:08:53,453
Just over half of all games use all 9 moves:
about 82 thousand X wins, plus 46,080 draws.

139
00:08:53,773 --> 00:08:57,806
Together, that's 255,168.

140
00:08:58,050 --> 00:09:00,461
And here's how it connects to nine
factorial.

141
00:09:00,781 --> 00:09:06,929
Remember, nine factorial counted each game
that ends on move 5 a total of 24 times,

142
00:09:06,929 --> 00:09:08,930
once for each ghost ending.

143
00:09:09,250 --> 00:09:14,114
A game ending on move 6 was counted 6 times,
and on move 7, twice.

144
00:09:14,434 --> 00:09:18,063
Games that end on move 8 or 9 have at most
one empty square,

145
00:09:18,063 --> 00:09:19,938
so they were counted just once.

146
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:23,272
Multiply each bar by its number, add them
up,

147
00:09:23,272 --> 00:09:26,018
and you get exactly nine factorial again.

148
00:09:28,334 --> 00:09:29,849
Who wins the most games?

149
00:09:30,169 --> 00:09:40,345
X wins 131,184 of them, O wins 77,904, and
46,080 are draws.

150
00:09:40,665 --> 00:09:43,683
X wins in more than half of all possible
games,

151
00:09:43,683 --> 00:09:46,766
because going first gives X more chances to
win.

152
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,195
One last puzzle.

153
00:09:48,515 --> 00:09:52,014
Which first move for X leads to the most
different games:

154
00:09:52,014 --> 00:09:54,040
a corner, an edge, or the center?

155
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:55,448
Pause and guess.

156
00:10:05,684 --> 00:10:10,356
Surprise: the edge, with 29,592 games.

157
00:10:10,676 --> 00:10:19,721
Each corner gives 27,732, and the center the
fewest, just 25,872. Why?

158
00:10:20,041 --> 00:10:24,927
The center sits on 4 winning lines, a corner
on 3, and an edge on just 2.

159
00:10:25,247 --> 00:10:29,311
More lines give X more chances to win early,
on move 5 or 7,

160
00:10:29,311 --> 00:10:32,969
and every early win chops whole branches off
the tree.

161
00:10:33,217 --> 00:10:35,948
But careful: more games doesn't mean a
better move.

162
00:10:36,268 --> 00:10:40,534
Out of all the games that start in the
center, X wins about 6 in 10.

163
00:10:40,854 --> 00:10:43,521
Starting on an edge, X wins fewer than half.

164
00:10:45,567 --> 00:10:48,959
So does going first mean X always wins? No.

165
00:10:49,279 --> 00:10:51,071
A computer can do more than count.

166
00:10:51,391 --> 00:10:55,807
It starts at the bottom of the tree, where
every game is finished, and works upward.

167
00:10:56,127 --> 00:10:58,767
At every branch it picks the best move:

168
00:10:58,767 --> 00:11:02,356
best for X on X's turns, and best for O on
O's turns.

169
00:11:02,676 --> 00:11:05,142
Doing that, it has solved tic-tac-toe:

170
00:11:05,142 --> 00:11:08,906
if both players play perfectly, every game
ends in a draw.

171
00:11:09,226 --> 00:11:12,959
So every one of X's wins needs a mistake by
O somewhere.

172
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:17,021
Could we do the same for chess?

173
00:11:17,341 --> 00:11:18,365
Not a chance.

174
00:11:18,685 --> 00:11:23,561
In 1950, the mathematician and engineer
Claude Shannon estimated

175
00:11:23,561 --> 00:11:28,285
that chess has at least 10 to the power of
120 possible games.

176
00:11:28,605 --> 00:11:31,357
That's a 1 followed by 120 zeros!

177
00:11:31,677 --> 00:11:36,584
The whole observable universe has only about
10 to the power of 80 atoms.

178
00:11:36,834 --> 00:11:39,501
So chess programs can't explore every
branch.

179
00:11:39,821 --> 00:11:43,587
They look only so many moves ahead, then
estimate who's winning,

180
00:11:43,587 --> 00:11:46,882
using what they learned from millions of
practice games.

181
00:11:47,202 --> 00:11:50,473
But underneath, it's the same idea you just
learned:

182
00:11:50,473 --> 00:11:53,431
a tree of moves, explored one branch at a
time.

183
00:11:53,967 --> 00:11:54,970
Let's recap.

184
00:11:55,290 --> 00:12:01,903
Multiplying the choices gives nine
factorial, 362,880 orders.

185
00:12:02,223 --> 00:12:06,426
But games stop when someone wins, so many of
those orders are ghost games.

186
00:12:06,746 --> 00:12:11,334
And a short recursive program counts every
real game by trying a move,

187
00:12:11,334 --> 00:12:13,039
exploring, and undoing it.

188
00:12:13,359 --> 00:12:18,095
The answer is exactly 255,168.

189
00:12:18,334 --> 00:12:19,529
Now it's your turn.

190
00:12:19,849 --> 00:12:23,548
Change the program so it counts only the
games X wins,

191
00:12:23,548 --> 00:12:28,617
or only the games that end on move 7, or see
what happens if O goes first.

192
00:12:28,937 --> 00:12:32,393
The program and the challenges are linked in
the description.

193
00:12:32,713 --> 00:12:34,035
Have fun exploring!

